    yield ctx.registry.register("bottle_down", VERSION, BottleDown)
    yield ctx.registry.register("bottle_lift", VERSION, BottleLift)
    yield ctx.registry.register("bottle_above", VERSION, BottleAbove)
    yield ctx.registry.register("bottle_place", VERSION, BottlePlace)

    yield ctx.registry.register("retreat", VERSION, Retreat)

    yield ctx.registry.register("bowl_pick_vla", VERSION, BowlPickVLA)
    yield ctx.registry.register("turn_on_stove", VERSION, TurnOnStove)

    yield ctx.registry.register("main", VERSION, build)
```

Key changes: bottle pick stays geometric (proven); both places now **approach high, descend until the arm stalls, then release anyway** (so a 5–8 cm controller residual still lands the object on the region); the bowl is approached from the −x side where the arm moves freely, with two grasp depths and a VLA fallback; and the stove is turned on only after `bowl_ok` is verified.

Diagnosis across runs: `bowl_hover` reports success only because TOL is loose (it stalls ~6 cm short), `bowl_down`/`bowl_prep` move in **z only** (x never changes — the eef cannot move past x ≈ −0.07 at y ≈ 0, z ≈ 1.0, so the bowl at x = −0.10 and the rack at x = −0.27 are unreachable by straight-line moves with the kept orientation); the VLA approaches but never closes on the bowl. So the VLA is the only tool that can *re-approach*, and geometric placement may still work because the stove/rack sit off-axis (y ≠ 0).

Plan v14: cheap geometric attempts first (failing fast, capped step budgets), then **VLA pick with retries** + geometric place using the rigid offset measured while held, then a whole-task VLA per object, stove **only after** the bowl is verified on the cook region.

